  title="The Red Carpet"
  subtitle="The oldest ritual there is, dressed up in flashbulbs."
  description="The path once reserved for gods, now walked by mortals while the crowd adores."
>
  <p class="lede">The red carpet is not glamour. It is a confession.</p>

  <p>Its first appearance in all of literature is in the Greek tragedy <em>Agamemnon</em>, written by Aeschylus in 458 BC. A conquering king returns home from war, and a crimson path is unrolled from his chariot to his door. But the king hesitates to set his foot on it. In that world red was a <strong>divine</strong> color, and a path like this was <strong>reserved for the gods</strong> — to walk it as a mortal was to reach for an honor that belonged to deity alone, and he feared it.</p>

  <p>Read that again, because the whole meaning of the custom is right there in its very first use: <em>to walk the crimson path was to accept the worship owed to a god.</em></p>

  <p>Nothing has changed since but the guest list. The chariot became a limousine and the crimson wool became nylon, but the act is identical — a mortal steps onto the path built for gods, and an adoring crowd cheers him across it. We even keep the old word out loud: we call them <strong>stars</strong>, and we say we <em>adore</em> them.</p>

  <p>This is the oldest ceremony there is, and Scripture named the sin long before there was a camera pointed down a rope line:</p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>“Who changed the truth of Yahuah into a lie, and worshipped and served the creature more than the Creator, who is blessed for ever.”</p>
    <cite>Romans 1:25</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <p>The red carpet is that verse laid out in fabric — a path fit for the Creator, walked by the creature, while the crowd hands the creature the honor. The lights are new; the exchange is as old as Eden.</p>

  <p>And Scripture staged this exact scene once, then told us how it ends. A king named Herod put on his royal robes, took his seat on a raised platform, and made a speech to a gathered crowd — and the crowd roared back:</p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>“It is the voice of a god, and not of a man.”</p>
    <cite>Acts 12:22</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <p>There is the red carpet in a single sentence: a robed man on a raised place, and a crowd naming him a god. And notice exactly what Scripture says the crime was. It was not the applause itself — it was that Herod <em>“gave not Yahuah the glory”</em> (Acts 12:23). He kept the worship instead of turning it upward, and in that instant he was struck down. He accepted the honor that is only ever meant to travel one direction.</p>

  <p>That direction is the line the whole ceremony is built to blur — because Yahuah drew it Himself, and He will not move it:</p>

  <blockquote>
    <p>“I am Yahuah: that is my name: and my glory will I not give to another.”</p>
    <cite>Isaiah 42:8</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <p>The red carpet is the ceremony of handing it to another anyway. Every walk down it is Herod’s set day again — the robes, the platform, the roar of the crowd — with only the judgment held back, for now.</p>

